WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.890
Imagine a version of Earth where mass extinction
events occur like clockwork.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:23.770
Where every fifty-million years, solar storms
wipe out the majority of life, and the survivors

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.460
must start again.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.609
From such a relentless cycle of destruction
and renewal, what sorts of species might rise

00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:32.670
to the challenge?

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:38.330
Nijin-Konai is a speculative biology project
set on a planet where periods of intense solar

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:42.519
radiation constantly reset the timeline on
evolution.

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:47.930
As a result, the lifeforms that now populate
this world are true survivors, the product

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.450
of countless generations of mass extinction
and rebirth.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:57.789
Brought to life by the talented artist and
creator Lorenzo Battilani, the world is rich

00:00:57.789 --> 00:00:59.969
in biological detail.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:05.510
Indeed, this might be one of the most ambitious
speculative biology projects I’ve ever covered

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:10.630
— comprising hundreds of species, with thousands
of details about each one.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:16.200
The first volume the artist is creating focuses
on the aquatic life, so for this entry into

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.240
the archive, we’ll explore the seas of this
incredible world.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.090
So, let’s submerge ourselves in the waters
of Nijin-Konai, staying alert to what lurks

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:32.640
in the depths as we discover a world beneath
the waves...

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.350
The ozone layer of Nijin-Konai’s atmosphere
is extremely thin, leaving the planet at the

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.460
mercy of solar radiation surges.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.670
In between the surges, life rebound.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.790
Just thirty-two million years after the most
recent mass extinction, the latest inheritors

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:55.660
of Nijin-Konai have already achieved incredible
diversity throughout the oceans.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:01.540
In the midwater currents swims the ubiquitous
Spotted Snake-Jetter, a creature that possesses

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.210
a surprising adaptation.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.820
They ingest their food whole, storing their
meals in an enlargeable sac.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:14.360
Like the Gulper Eels of Earth, this sac can
swell up to a size as big as the animal itself

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.680
depending on the size of their most recent
mouthful — resulting in a rather absurd

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.680
spectacle.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.290
The Spotted Snake-Jetter is a quintessential
example of the fish-like Pressionatatia — one

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.520
of the main lineages that have risen to prominence
in the current era.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.140
Yet the uniqueness of the Snake-Jetter shows
that on Nijin-Konai, even commonplace lifeforms

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.640
can be remarkable.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.990
Deeper in the open sea, other lifeforms have
found radically different niches to fill.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:46.209
The Purple Pillowhead inhabits low-current
environments, and has a peculiar method of

00:02:46.209 --> 00:02:47.209
getting food.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:53.420
Unlike the Snake-Jetter, this mid-sized lifeform
is a filter-feeder, possessing ten forward-facing

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.720
filtration organs in the frontal section of
their cranium.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.750
They spend their days filtering particulates
from the current, a feeding behavior similar

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.459
to Earth whale sharks.

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:08.580
The taxon they represent is composed primarily
of filter feeders, which are finding great

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.989
success on the nutrient-rich currents of Nijin-Konai’s
oceans.

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:17.940
In looking at such vibrant forms of life,
there’s a strange comfort that these populations

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.180
are the remainders of a mass extinction…

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.710
In the reef-like environments of warmer waters,
surreal forms of life seek refuge.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.640
The Butterfly Chichi is one such species.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:36.060
A rather tiny organism, their trunk-like appendage
gives them an unmistakable look.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:41.709
The Butterfly Chichi use their miniature trunk
to bring small pieces of vegetation into their

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:42.709
mouths.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:46.310
And though you’d never know it by looking
at them, the Butterfly Chichi is the last

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.730
herbivore of a great line.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:53.769
Once part of an incredibly successful order
of megafauna, the latest extinction has left

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:56.200
the Butterfly Chichi all alone.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.459
Yet upon the reefs, they have endured, and
will continue to carry their taxon’s legacy

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:03.870
into the future.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:09.030
In the thick vegetation of the denser reefs,
a long, eel-like lifeform ungulates through

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.180
the water.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:15.540
This is a Rainbow Rockhead, and while they
might look threatening, they aren’t carnivores.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.880
Indeed, this filter-feeder is rather shy,
preferring to remain out of sight.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.410
Like the Moray Eel, the Rainbow Rockhead makes
their home in the cracks and crevices of the

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.750
coral reef, and spend most of their time hidden
from view.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:35.720
Yet a glimpse of a Rainbow Rockhead is a stunning
one, as they possess vibrant colors to attract

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:36.720
mates.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:43.880
While life on Nijin-Konai may be one of inevitable
struggle, beauty still finds a way to emerge.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.720
Yet all environments have their predators.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.080
The Doublejaw Kugawa is a lonesome hunter,
that will sometimes come to the reefs in search

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:53.520
of food.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.800
Possessing a unique jaw-apparatus for ensnaring
prey, the Kugawa can hunt a variety of prey

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:00.970
across the ocean.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:05.960
While typically solitary, once a year, the
Kugawa group together with others of their

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.550
kind in a school of unparalleled volume.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:14.759
In our oceans, schools of fish can reach amazing
sizes — with some stretching for several

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:15.850
miles.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:20.660
Yet during their migration to the tropics
of the north, the Kugawa school comprises

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:26.610
the species’ entire mature global population,
a sight that would be as alarming as it would

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.610
be stunning.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.030
And there is a giant upon the reef.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:37.220
This is a Sandmaker, and they have one of
the largest head-to-body ratios on the planet.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.230
Evolved to feed on the shelled plants that
make up the reefs they inhabit, they act as

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:45.400
a key contributor to the bioerosion processes
of the ocean.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:50.890
Each time they feed, they grind shells into
a fine sand, creating new sediment that helps

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:52.450
the reefs expand.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:58.060
Freshwater mollusks like Lithoredo Abatanica
play a similar role in underwater erosion

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:02.810
on our planet — grinding up limestone into
a fine-grained sand.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:08.280
Although at over twenty feet, or six meters
in length, the Sandmaker is a considerably

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.669
larger terraforming unit than an Earth mollusk.

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:16.569
Sizeable though they are, the Sandmakers are
gentle creatures, and one of the more notable

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:19.830
herbivores on Nijin-Konai…

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.460
Across the oceans, one of the more variable
clades are the frisbeefish.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:29.350
Radiating out from a common ancestor that
evolved a tall and flattened body to better

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:34.820
maneuver a densely vegetated area, the many
types of firsbeefish have returned to the

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.750
open ocean — and are returning to more hydrodynamic
shapes.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.979
Many of these emergent lineages look quite
distinctive, like the Heartfish — a species

00:06:43.979 --> 00:06:47.110
that, true to their name, mates for life.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.860
When a Heartfish couple bonds, the two court
each other by swirling around mid-water.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:57.210
It’s a rather poignant form of courtship,
and can be seen among other types of frisbeefish

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.240
as well.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.580
This is an Azure Frisbeefish.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:07.300
These hypnotic, six-finned oddities have evolved
to feed on floating fronds, which might explain

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.050
the design of their unusual fins.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.770
On our planet, certain genera of seahorses
and their relatives have evolved frond-like

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:18.250
protuberances to blend in among the plants
they feed on.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:23.430
So, it’s likely the Azure Frisbeefish’s
strange fins help break up their silhouette

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.590
amongst alien foliage.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:30.910
In any case, the Frisbeefish genus seems to
be thriving, as this species can now be found

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:34.210
across three-quarters of the planet’s oceans.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:38.759
Yet aquatic life on Nijin Konai isn’t limited
to just the oceans.

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:44.710
Along the banks of coastal rivers, a peculiar
customer is hiding in plain sight.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:50.880
This is a Freshwater Pebble-Bug, and they
are experts at looking like… well, pebbles.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.620
When other organisms get close, the Pebble-Bug
will quickly dig into the sediment to hide,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.330
leaving only their shells exposed.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:05.180
The unusual looking Scorpionfish of our oceans
have converged on a similar behavior to conceal

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.490
themselves in aquatic sediment.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:11.830
With only their shells sticking out, the Pebble-Bugs
look like any other rock.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:17.780
Yet the Pebble-Bug isn’t just designed to
hide from predators, but also to lure in prey.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:23.539
They possess an appendage that greatly resembles
a plant to attract small herbivorous animals,

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:26.139
which the Pebble-Bug will then ambush and
feed on.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:32.099
In a world this competitive, even the gentlest
looking species can be dangerous…

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:37.180
Back in the ocean, a relative of the Pebble-Bug
swims close of the sea floor.

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:42.060
This is a Grappling Shieldback, and like the
Pebble-Bug, they have a thick shell.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.920
Too heavy for fast swimming, the Shieldback
relies on their natural armor to keep them

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.050
safe from the jaws of larger predators.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:54.690
Like the Sea Turtles of earth, the Shieldback
must trust that their shells are tougher than

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:58.740
the teeth of most animals that swim under
the waves.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:05.000
Yet unlike the Sea Turtle, the Grappling Shieldback
is a predator themselves, using their prehensile

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.580
appendage to stir up sand and ensnare fleeing
bottom-feeders.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:14.570
It might seem strange that both the Shieldback
and the Pebble-bug are hunters.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:20.870
But both species are members of the Entodontia,
a once highly-successful order of mighty predators

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:25.330
that have been reduced to minor players following
the most recent extinction.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:32.170
In the world of Nijin-Konai few species stay
on top for long…

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:37.050
Yet some Entodontia have managed to remain
at the top of the food web.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:41.610
In the open sea, the Four-Jawed Irobian Ripper
hunts in the current.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:45.300
With a name like that, you know a creature
means business.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:50.980
Unlike their relatives, the Ripper possess
multiple vertically arranged jaws that they

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:52.860
use to latch on to prey.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:58.390
This unusual jaw structure might seem overly
elaborate, but many types of Earth crustaceans

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:02.430
possess similarly complex mouthparts for ensnaring
food.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:07.950
The Ripper’s extravagant mouth is actually
a sign of their primordial origins, as the

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.760
ripper is the rare lifeform that has changed
little over the past age.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:19.660
To have endured so long with minimal changes,
the Ripper’s strategy must be successful...

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:24.670
On the subject of successful hunters, peering
up from the sandy seafloor is the Phantom

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:26.640
Sea Twinfish.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.080
Resembling a fish flattened out by a rolling
pin, the Twinfish is an ambush predator that

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.760
appears… a bit squashed.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.740
Yet by lying flush with the seafloor, the
Twinfish is in the perfect position to feed

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:39.860
on other bottom-dwellers.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:45.570
It’s a strategy convergent with that of
the Spotted Wobbegong — a real name for

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:50.610
a real shark species that lies on the seafloor
and lets prey come to them.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:55.200
They’re not the most glamorous species next
to the sleek pursuit predators that are their

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:00.080
cousins, but the Spotted Wobbegong is one
shark that knows being fast isn’t the only

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.050
way to score a meal.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:07.660
Likewise, the Phantom Sea Twinfish is rather
slow — but as one of the only species in

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:10.329
their niche, there’s not much competition.

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:15.860
Indeed, the entire genus was formed through
an error in gene expression some 20 million

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:21.750
years ago — which helps make sense of the
Twinfish’s odd appearance...

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:26.920
Elsewhere on the seafloor, a seemingly terrifying
lifeform skulks.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:32.470
This serpentine predator is a Southern Sandlurker
— a creature that would be horrifying…

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.250
if they were more than a few centimeters in
length.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:41.470
The Sandlurker still stands out, however,
thanks to their unusual evolutionary history.

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:47.810
Like sea snakes, the Southern Sandlurker is
an aquatic descendant of a land-dwelling predecessor,

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:50.450
which made the transition quite recently.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:55.740
As a result, the Sandlurker still possesses
tiny limbs, which they use to peddle through

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.330
the water and snatch up their prey.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:02.240
The result is a creature that looks… a little
uncanny.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:06.290
And disturbingly, there is another offshoot
of the Sandlurker.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:12.070
This photo shows an exceedingly rare Tooth
Collector, a Sandlurker that picks shed teeth

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:15.589
up from the seafloor to ingest for calcium
intake.

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:20.670
Not only is this behavior off-putting, the
Tooth Collector seems larger than their southern

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:21.670
cousins.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.790
A rather unsettling discovery...

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:32.160
Much further to the north, the arctic regions
of Nijin-Konai are a land of ice and wind.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.700
Yet here too, life has found a way to thrive.

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:40.930
This is an Arctic Wildmillworm, a creature
that can only be found thousands of meters

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:42.470
below the ice.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:47.209
Living in large colonies upon the seabed,
Wildmillworms are one of the more extreme

00:12:47.209 --> 00:12:50.279
creatures to emerge upon this planet.

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:55.850
Believe it or not, Arctic Worms also swim
under Earth’s polar ice caps — prospering

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:59.040
in the pitch-black despite the glacial temperatures.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.260
Likewise, the Wildmillworm is right at home
in their environment, able to feed thanks

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:06.990
to filters on their four-part mouth.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:12.240
These distinctive structures actually rotate
in the arctic current like a windmill, allowing

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:17.709
the worms to catch more food and even direct
currents towards other members of the colony.

00:13:17.709 --> 00:13:22.310
A truly ingenious product of adaptation.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:27.980
Elsewhere under the ice, a hunter with a curious
swimming posture is waiting for prey.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:33.570
This is a Dusky Traptongue, and they eke out
a peculiar existence indeed.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:38.040
Swimming with their mouth positioned upwards,
The Traptounge spends much of their life waiting

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.320
for prey to pass overhead.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.509
Their posture resembles the amusing looking
Pipefish, which — like the Dusky Traptongue

00:13:45.509 --> 00:13:49.140
— have a highly unusual vertical swimming
position.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:53.839
You or I might become uncomfortable with our
necks permanently craned upwards.

00:13:53.839 --> 00:13:58.720
But the Traptounge is quite literally born
for this, having evolved a large stabilizing

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:03.660
fin and vertical countershading that helps
them remain camouflaged while in their strange

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.120
hunting position.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.660
At least this oddity won’t be uncomfortable...

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:12.830
Few undersea environments are more extreme
than the polar regions.

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:18.850
Yet in the crushing depths of the planet’s
trenches, conditions are even more hostile.

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:24.650
In the twilight zone of the abyss, the Crimson
Knifefish flashes a fanged smile.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:29.860
This deep-sea predator lives near volcanic
fault lines, and snatches up fish that come

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:31.990
to feed on the chemical soup.

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:37.130
The structure on the front of its head resembles
the lure of certain members of the Anglerfish

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:38.180
family.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:42.800
The Knifefish’s structure isn’t a lure,
however, but a deadly weapon.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:48.360
Made of hardened bone, the Knifefish uses
this horn as a biological blade — impaling

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.589
fast moving prey with pinpoint accuracy.

00:14:51.589 --> 00:14:56.680
In the deep sea, staying alive is a constant
battle…

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:01.860
A titanic predator inhabits the deep abyssal
plains of the cold trenches.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.340
The Grim Walker.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:08.600
Instead of swimming, the creature has evolved
to walk on the ocean floor using their six

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:09.940
lower fins.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:15.910
An apex predator, the Grim Walker hunts using
their large mouths to suck in prey — grinding

00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:19.460
them alive within their secondary rows of
teeth.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:24.870
On Earth, the Warty Frogfish is an aquatic
organism that also ‘walks’ on the sea

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:25.870
floor…

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:30.870
and they have a similarly horrific appetite,
able to vacuum up prey almost twice their

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:31.970
size.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:36.490
The ghostly white eyes of the Grim Walker
betray their poor vision, as they seek out

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:39.690
food in the dark abyss using echolocation
alone.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:45.290
And since the largest Grim Walkers can reach
almost thirty feet, or nine meters in length,

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:50.899
they’re a serious threat — known to swallow
diving equipment like camera drones, and even

00:15:50.899 --> 00:15:53.529
to attack small submersibles when hungry.

00:15:53.529 --> 00:15:59.180
For this reason, the Grim Walkers are best
observed from a distance.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:05.610
Speaking of which, in the dark recess of Nijin-Konai,
a disturbing presence haunts the void.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:11.769
Little understood, Giant Ghostfaces are known
to be swift and silent, grabbing unaware prey

00:16:11.769 --> 00:16:14.120
with their four long arms.

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:18.889
In the few images of this seldom glimpsed
lifeform, their appearance is like something

00:16:18.889 --> 00:16:20.540
from a bad dream.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:24.850
Perhaps some regions of Nijin-Konai, are better
left unexamined…

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:28.360
But let’s end with a moment of beauty.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:33.190
In the calm blue of the open ocean, one final
lifeform is waiting.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.470
This is a Painted Krillnet, one of the largest
species that swim beneath the waves.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:44.980
A leviathan of the deep, the Painted Krillnet
grows to vast sizes on a diet of phytoplankton,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:49.570
which they harvest through the specialized
hair-like filaments on their frontal jaws.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:56.050
Like a baleen whale, the Krillnet can consume
several tons of these organisms every day.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:17:01.470
As a result of their rich diet, the Painted
Krillnet possesses a vivid coloration, which

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.880
plays an important role in their mating rituals.

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:10.230
The emergence of organisms this massive and
complex in just a few million years proves

00:17:10.230 --> 00:17:17.980
— one last time — how quickly life can
fill a void following a mass extinction…

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:22.280
In the end, the story of Nijin-Konai is more
than one of extinction.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:27.850
Not only has life on the planet recovered
in spectacular fashion, but the world itself

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:31.370
is far more detailed than I could ever cover
in this video.

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:36.670
A larger science fiction story involving human
settlers is emerging through the author’s

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:40.650
art, which you can explore more of in the
links in the description.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:45.990
If you enjoyed this glimpse into the larger
world of Nijin-Konai, please consider following

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:48.210
and supporting the artist.

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.200
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:56.470
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:59.799
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:17:59.799 --> 00:18:09.100
See you in the next video.

